{:edb/db-before-t 2703 :edb/db-after-t 2704 :edb/tx-data [[13194139536016 50 #inst "2026-10-09T12:59:46.009Z" 13194139536016 true] [17592186197941 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/a8d3e8c54d61326d9f7d5203d32bd48006c404e9d3990820cb8884c08df8dba3.png)\n\nChange the order of integration in the integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\n\\int_0^1 \\int_0^{2} \\int_0^{\\sqrt[3]{z}} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}z\n$$\n\nso that it is integrated first with respect to $z,$ then over a region $D$ in the $xy$-plane. The integration region $R$ is shown above." 13194139536016 true] [17592186197941 1100 "Change the order of integration in the integral\n$\\displaystyle \\int_0^1 \\int_0^{2} \\int_0^{\\sqrt[3]{z}} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}z$\nso that it is integrated first with respect to $z$, then over a region $D$ in the $xy$-plane. The integration region $R$ is shown above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4158/Source/Images/dc30042ba8467a4dd3b57e4729cf275e.png)" 13194139536016 false] [17592186197941 1145 "We wish to find the limits of integration $u_1(x,y)$ and $u_2(x,y)$ such that\n\n$$\n\\int_0^1 \\int_0^{2} \\int_0^{\\sqrt[3]{z}}  f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}z\n=\n\\iint\\limits_D \\left[ \\int_{u_1(x,y)}^{u_2(x,y)} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}z\\right] \\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the region of integration $R$ can be written as a type III region as follows:\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{\n(x,y,z) \\:\\: {:} \\:\\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq \\sqrt[3]{z} \n\\big\\}\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}z$ to $\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}A,$ we write $R$ as a type I region.\n\nTo visualize this, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $z$-axis and in the direction of increasing $z,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/efc1013a8202f8eea26250b904cf3d4672b30d478ddaad72b6347b274bd33f7d.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the bottom boundary and leaves through the top boundary:\n\n- The bottom boundary of the region, written in the form $z=z(x,y),$ is\n\n$$\ny = \\sqrt[3]{z}, \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nz = y^3.\n$$\n\n- The top boundary of the region is $z = 1.$\n\nTherefore, the type I representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{\n(x,y,z) \\:\\: {:} \\:\\: (x,y) \\in D, \\:\\: y^3\\leq z \\leq 1\n\\big\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/3ae61409e70d4d6d6227a306ab1651331d9b40633248fd525cefbab519202a52.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration so that it is integrated first with respect to $z$ and then over $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\int_0^1 \\int_0^{2} \\int_0^{\\sqrt[3]{z}} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}z\n=\n\\iint\\limits_D \\left[\\int_{y^3}^{1} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}z\\right] \\textrm{d}A.\n$$" 13194139536016 true] [17592186197941 1145 "We wish to find the limits of integration $u_1(x,y)$ and $u_2(x,y)$ such that\n\n$$\n\\int_0^1 \\int_0^{2} \\int_0^{\\sqrt[3]{z}} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}z = \\iint\\limits_D \\left[ \\int_{u_1(x,y)}^{u_2(x,y)} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}z\\right] \\textrm{d}A\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the region of integration $R$ can be written as a type III region as follows:\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{(x,y,z) \\:\\: {:} \\:\\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq \\sqrt[3]{z} \\big\\}\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}z$ to $\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}A$, we write $R$ as a type I region.\n\nTo visualize this, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $z$-axis and in the direction of increasing $z$, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4158/Source/Images/20ba647fd3e3402dc4ba57a1efe199b3.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the bottom boundary and leaves through the top boundary:\n\n- The bottom boundary of the region, written in the form $z=z(x,y)$, is\n$y = \\sqrt[3]{z}, \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad z = y^3$.\n- The top boundary of the region is $z = 1$.\n\nTherefore, the type I representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{(x,y,z) \\:\\: {:} \\:\\: (x,y) \\in D, \\:\\: y^3\\leq z \\leq 1 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4158/Source/Images/54727f66b4a3d3b312c032189ed08a48.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration so that it is integrated first with respect to $z$ and then over $D$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\int_0^1 \\int_0^{2} \\int_0^{\\sqrt[3]{z}} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}z = \\iint\\limits_D \\left[\\int_{y^3}^{1} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}z\\right] \\textrm{d}A\n$$" 13194139536016 false] [17592186197942 1100 "Change the order of integration in the integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\n\\int_0^{1} \\int_0^{1} \\int_0^{1-z^2} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nso that it is integrated first with respect to $z,$ then $y,$ and then $x.$" 13194139536016 true] [17592186197942 1100 "Change the order of integration in the integral\n$\\displaystyle \\int_0^{1} \\int_0^{1} \\int_0^{1-z^2} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}x$.\nso that it is integrated first with respect to $z$, then $y$, and then $x$." 13194139536016 false] [17592186197942 1145 "We wish to find limits of integration $u_1(x,y)$, $u_2(x,y)$, $v_1(x)$, $v_2(x)$, $a$, and $b$ such that\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{1} \\int_0^{1} \\int_0^{1-z^2} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}x = \\int_a^b \\int_{v_1(x)}^{v_2(x)} \\int_{u_1(x,y)}^{u_2(x,y)} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the region of integration $R$ can be written as a type III region as follows:\n\n$$\nR = \\left\\{(x,y,z) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1-z^2 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch the region. Since it is a type III region, we first draw the projection onto the $xz$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nE = \\left\\{(x,z) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 1 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4158/Source/Images/0898e2ab1dad983d6db2c113b25a7b9b.png)\n\nThen, the region $R$ is shown in the diagram below, where\n\n$$\n0 \\leq y \\leq 1-z^2\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4158/Source/Images/7bf3173bf663a363a02d25d6025c92dc.png)\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}x$ to $\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x$, we write $R$ as a type I region and its projection $D$ onto the $xy$-plane as a type I *plane* region.\n\nTo visualize this, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $z$-axis and in the direction of increasing $z$, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4158/Source/Images/54b1cbed41d7992355278fe388a141c6.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the bottom boundary and leaves through the top boundary:\n\n- The top boundary of the region, written in the form $z=z(x,y)$, is\n$y = 1-z^2 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad z = \\sqrt{1- y}$.\n- The bottom boundary of the region is $z=0$.\n\nTherefore, the type I representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{(x,y,z) \\:: \\: (x,y) \\in D, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq \\sqrt{1-y} \\big\\}\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4158/Source/Images/5a04ed84a421e394756b01cbdd473210.png)\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ as a type I plane region in the $xy$-plane with the top curve given by $y=1$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4158/Source/Images/84fb222f81aaade0074f70c558fa3b30.png)\n\nHence, we have that\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration so that it is integrated first with respect to $z$, then $y$, then $x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{1} \\int_0^{1} \\int_0^{1-z^2} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}x = \\int_0^{1} \\int_0^{\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{1}} \\int_{\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{0}}^{\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\sqrt{1-y}}} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$" 13194139536016 false] [17592186197942 1145 "We wish to find limits of integration $u_1(x,y),$ $u_2(x,y),$ $v_1(x),$ $v_2(x),$ $a,$ and $b$ such that\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{1} \\int_0^{1} \\int_0^{1-z^2} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}x\n=\n\\int_a^b \\int_{v_1(x)}^{v_2(x)} \\int_{u_1(x,y)}^{u_2(x,y)} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the region of integration $R$ can be written as a type III region as follows:\n\n$$\nR = \\left\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1-z^2 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch the region. Since it is a type III region, we first draw the projection onto the $xz$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nE = \\left\\{\n(x,z) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 1\n\\right\\}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/8007b0f7348f9855a009aff56c676122a739e6210d4f024c524e72e3cfa23312.png)\n\nThen, the region $R$ is shown in the diagram below, where $0 \\leq y \\leq  1-z^2.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/97d0f5fc56498e1a044dbbfc07da571c1806903822dfdb1d68f877723ce9a559.png)\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}x$ to $\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x,$ we write $R$ as a type I region and its projection $D$ onto the $xy$-plane as a type I *plane* region.\n\nTo visualize this, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $z$-axis and in the direction of increasing $z,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/f5fab194951b50a2a0c81aff8d422b98be5145bbef3123ea746b86bcebd1e778.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the bottom boundary and leaves through the top boundary:\n\n- The top boundary of the region, written in the form $z=z(x,y),$ is\n\n$$\ny = 1-z^2\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nz = \\sqrt{1- y}.\n$$\n\n- The bottom boundary of the region is $z=0.$\n\nTherefore, the type I representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\: (x,y) \\in D, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq \\sqrt{1-y} \\big\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/403675d42fae7e437e62bcb3e254e3f7cf3997556606ed529807d83253d1c439.png)\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ as a type I plane region in the $xy$-plane with the top curve given by $y=1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/1e1fbae22c5ad39225a5de4e3c3aeb025cb41b21bbf0c127da57e3ada10c3087.png)\n\nHence, we have that\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1 \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration so that it is integrated first with respect to $z,$ then $y,$ then $x,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{1} \\int_0^{1} \\int_0^{1-z^2} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}x\n=\n\\int_0^{1} \\int_0^{\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{1}} \\int_{\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{0}}^{\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\sqrt{1-y}}} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x.\n$$" 13194139536016 true] [17592186197966 1100 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1} \\int_{0}^{2} \\int_{z}^{2} e^{x^2} \\, \\mathrm{d}x \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\: \\mathrm{d}y$.\n\n*Hint: Change the order of integration to $\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}y$.*" 13194139536016 false] [17592186197966 1100 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1} \\int_{0}^{2} \\int_{z}^{2} e^{x^2} \\, \\mathrm{d}x \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\: \\mathrm{d}y.$\n\n*Hint: Change the order of integration to $\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}y.$*" 13194139536016 true] [17592186197966 1145 "We cannot integrate the given integral because we cannot find a simple antiderivative of the integrand. So, we change the order of integration.\n\nWe wish to find the limits of integration $u_1(x,y)$, $u_2(x,y)$, $v_1(y)$, $v_2(y)$, $a$, and $b$ such that\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{1} \\int_{0}^{2} \\int_{z}^{2} e^{x^2} \\, \\mathrm{d}x \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\: \\mathrm{d}y = \\int_{a}^{b} \\int_{v_1(y)}^{v_2(y)} \\int_{u_1(x,y)}^{u_2(x,y)} e^{x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}z \\: \\textrm{d}x \\: \\textrm{d}y\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the region of integration $R$ can be written as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nR = \\left\\{(x,y,z) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 2, \\:\\: z \\leq x \\leq 2 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch the region. Since it is a type II region, we first sketch the projection onto the $yz$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nE = \\left\\{(y,z) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 2 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4158/Source/Images/50867af2658a2fd8436bc8f1c49b01d9.png)\n\nThen, the region $R$ is shown in the diagram below, where\n\n$$\nz \\leq x \\leq 2\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4158/Source/Images/183a99ad9f325c9c75801f18e667d562.png)\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\mathrm{d}x \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\: \\mathrm{d}y$ to $\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}y$, we write $R$ as a type I region and its projection $D$ onto the $xy$-plane as a type II *plane* region.\n\nTo visualize this, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $z$-axis and in the direction of increasing $z$, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4158/Source/Images/0f5cb134455e0cfceef74be513903e4a.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the bottom boundary and leaves through the top boundary:\n\n- The bottom boundary of the region is\n$z=0$.\n- The top boundary of the region, written in the form $z = z(x,y)$, is\n$x = z \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad z = x$.\n\nTherefore, the type I representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR = \\left\\{(x,y,z) \\:: \\: (x,y) \\in D, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq x \\right\\}\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4158/Source/Images/e4de33214185ed8a7bebf8a9db1b1051.png)\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ as a type II plane region in the $xy$-plane with the left curve given by $x = 0$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4158/Source/Images/8b095ecbfe344bc149ece073098d6a15.png)\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\:\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{2}∫_{z}^{2}e^{x^{2}}dxdzdy &= ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{2}∫_{0}^{x}e^{x^{2}}dzdxdy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{2}e^{x^{2}}[∫_{0}^{x}dz]dxdy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{2}e^{x^{2}}[z]_{0}^{x}dxdy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{2}xe^{x^{2}}dxdy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}[∫_{0}^{2}xe^{x^{2}}dx]dy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}[\\frac{1}{2}e^{x^{2}}]_{0}^{2}dy \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}∫_{0}^{1}(e^{4} - 1)dy \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(e^{4} - 1) \\cdot [y]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(e^{4} - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536016 false] [17592186197966 1145 "We cannot integrate the given integral because we cannot find a simple antiderivative of the integrand. So, we change the order of integration.\n\nWe wish to find the limits of integration $u_1(x,y),$ $u_2(x,y),$ $v_1(y),$ $v_2(y),$ $a,$ and $b$ such that\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{1} \\int_{0}^{2} \\int_{z}^{2} e^{x^2} \\, \\mathrm{d}x \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\: \\mathrm{d}y\n=\n\\int_{a}^{b} \\int_{v_1(y)}^{v_2(y)} \\int_{u_1(x,y)}^{u_2(x,y)} e^{x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}z \\: \\textrm{d}x \\: \\textrm{d}y.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the region of integration $R$ can be written as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nR = \\left\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 2, \\:\\: z \\leq x \\leq 2 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch the region. Since it is a type II region, we first sketch the projection onto the $yz$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nE = \\left\\{ (y,z) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 2 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/9fe61cd50d31086b47495f0bd0fd71308fc30286d392f4d0064cf743b93272b0.png)\n\nThen, the region $R$ is shown in the diagram below, where $z \\leq x \\leq 2.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/6af345af2d33b53c698e8e1d4eff10e3b3116911e14ee1b4581da7eee8528f94.png)\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\mathrm{d}x \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\: \\mathrm{d}y$ to $\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}y,$ we write $R$ as a type I region and its projection $D$ onto the $xy$-plane as a type II *plane* region.\n\nTo visualize this, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $z$-axis and in the direction of increasing $z,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/53ec4e56ca38362b10c05d4cc657f9057005d9692e0fcde1f0846e7e80b4f478.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the bottom boundary and leaves through the top boundary:\n\n- The bottom boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nz=0.\n$$\n\n- The top boundary of the region, written in the form $z = z(x,y),$ is\n\n$$\nx = z \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad z = x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the type I representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR = \\left\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\: (x,y) \\in D, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq x \\right\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/5ce67f398f60237d763d393d62f9e7346270b954acc7efdc7a096a5f3155f4d3.png)\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ as a type II plane region in the $xy$-plane with the left curve given by $x = 0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/0dbf7a35fe74730db7c2a07fc4bbe881a66412cee53bf0a606c522f5493e58c2.png)\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\:\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1 \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{z}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,dx\\,dz\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,dz\\,dx\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{2}}[{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\text{d}z]\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{2}}[z]_{0}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}x{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{{x}^{2}}]}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{4}-1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{4}-1)\\cdot [y]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{4}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536016 true] [17592186198298 1020 #edb/ref 17592186379505 13194139536016 true] [17592186198298 1020 #edb/ref 17592186379512 13194139536016 true] [17592186198298 1020 #edb/ref 17592186379519 13194139536016 tru