ssets/4429082d4ab0d6fdef75d79b1db02944f0c53741fb0ccf19d8a4ef46a253698f.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the left boundary and leaves through the right boundary:\n\n- The right boundary of the region, written in the form $y=(x,z),$ is\n\n$$\nz=\\sqrt{y}\\,⟹\\,y={z}^{2}\n$$\n\n- The left boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the type III representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le {z}^{2}},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/a941e1bfabb21561b622080775c3c5389a86cf4150f1fb28dd692a022f607620.png)\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ as a type I plane region in the $xz$-plane with the top curve given by $z=\\sqrt{1}=1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/20e339260b0947f92d8fedfa47e899ed53753e03e30c4b816edec06d092d9a01.png)\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y}}^{1}{e}^{{z}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{z}^{2}}{e}^{{z}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{{z}^{3}}[{\\int }_{0}^{{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{{z}^{3}}{[y]}_{0}^{{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{z}^{2}\\,{e}^{{z}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}{z}^{2}\\,{e}^{{z}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}z]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{{e}^{{z}^{3}}}{3}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{e-1}{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{e-1}{3}[x]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{e-1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536016 true] [17592186198897 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139536016 true] [17592186198897 1100 "${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{\\pi }}{\\int }_{{z}^{2}}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n*Hint: Change the order of integration to $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.$*" 13194139536016 true] [17592186198897 1100 "$∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{π}}∫_{z^{2}}^{\\sqrt{π}}z\\cos (\\frac{π}{6} + y^{2})dydzdx =$\n\n*Hint: Change the order of integration to $dzdydx$.*" 13194139536016 false] [17592186198897 1145 "We cannot integrate the given integral because we cannot find a simple antiderivative of the integrand. So, we change the order of integration.\n\nWe wish to find the limits of integration ${u}_{1}(x,y),$ ${u}_{2}(x,y),$ ${v}_{1}(x),$ ${v}_{2}(x),$ $a,$ and $b$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{\\pi }}{\\int }_{{z}^{2}}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(x)}^{{v}_{2}(x)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,y)}^{{u}_{2}(x,y)}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the region of integration $R$ can be written as a type III region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt[4]{\\pi },\\,\\,{z}^{2}\\le y\\le \\sqrt{\\pi }}\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch the region. Since it is a type III region, we first sketch the projection onto the $xz$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nE={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt[4]{\\pi }}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/3bb477b93fb92174307bee87130d9957b135cdafaa4167f78f3d603c4192391d.png)\n\nThen, the region $R$ is shown in the diagram below, where ${z}^{2}\\le y\\le \\sqrt{\\pi }.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/cf966e9ec5fbcf11727d46bb3906f05e4b30df4c9bef86a6eab4850b967d7ba0.png)\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x$ to $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x,$ we write $R$ as a type I region and its projection $D$ onto the $xy$-plane as a type I *plane* region.\n\nTo visualize this, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $z$-axis and in the direction of increasing $z,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/88b5e19c3e263d160db8d041fd3504b25d4a8a8f7ae8e111cdeb5b153c3cd21f.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the bottom boundary and leaves through the top boundary:\n\n- The top boundary of the region, written in the form $z=z(x,y),$ is\n\n$$\ny={z}^{2}\\,⟺\\,z=\\sqrt{y}.\n$$\n\n- The bottom boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nz=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the type I representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt{y}},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/b748578888fdda878b0d44bb036310644c3437c1922aafd49cab6c165a9cf132.png)\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ as a type I plane region in the $xy$-plane with the top curve given by $y=\\sqrt{\\pi }.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/60e35d0e77314aaecc60b0eda3c039afa0bdd44d49f68ad39e27c8856a9390e0.png)\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{\\pi }}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{\\pi }}{\\int }_{{z}^{2}}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+{y}^{2})[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}z\\,\\text{d}z]\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+{y}^{2}){[\\frac{{z}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\frac{y}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\frac{y}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+{y}^{2})]}_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}))\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}-\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{x}{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379505 1017 #edb/ref 17592186379506 13194139536016 true] [17592186379505 1089 "q-162398" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379505 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536016 true] [17592186379505 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/9f4f24dd100758466d8a06eee113bada8b1bede4b48c0a3e694e4c1e3f1c2371.png)\n\nChange the order of integration in the integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x/4}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x/4-z}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nso that it is integrated first with respect to $z,$ then over a region $D$ in the $xy$-plane. The integration region $R$ is shown above." 13194139536016 true] [17592186379505 1113 #uuid "ef46d483-e881-585c-a3d4-01fb7fd607a4" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379505 1145 "We wish to find the limits of integration ${u}_{1}(y,z)$ and ${u}_{2}(y,z)$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x/4}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x/4-z}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,y)}^{{u}_{2}(x,y)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z]\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the region of integration $R$ can be written as a type III region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 4,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1-\\frac{x}{4},\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1-\\frac{x}{4}-z}\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x$ to $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}A,$ we write $R$ as a type I region. \n\nTo visualize this, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $z$-axis and in the direction of increasing $z,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/dc1a9b6371456f635cd4b73fe99301fa349ceeec1341b9bd3b86f6bcd7ad8ba2.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the bottom boundary and leaves through the top boundary:\n\n- The top boundary of the region, written in the form $z=z(x,y),$ is\n\n$$\ny=1-\\frac{x}{4}-z\\,⟹\\,z=1-\\frac{x}{4}-y.\n$$\n\n- The bottom boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nz=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the type I representation of $R$ is \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1-\\frac{x}{4}-y},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/07c4ee04dfba186348b3bd56b182477cec9ca1a581e37bb0eb4ab9a3d64ee515.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration so that it is integrated first with respect to $z$ and then over $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x/4}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x/4-z}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x & ={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{1-x/4-y}f(x,y,z)\\,dz]dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379506 1000 #edb/ref 17592186379507 13194139536016 true] [17592186379506 1000 #edb/ref 17592186379508 13194139536016 true] [17592186379506 1000 #edb/ref 17592186379509 13194139536016 true] [17592186379506 1000 #edb/ref 17592186379510 13194139536016 true] [17592186379506 1000 #edb/ref 17592186379511 13194139536016 true] [17592186379506 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536016 true] [17592186379506 1041 "selection" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379506 1104 #edb/ref 17592186379510 13194139536016 true] [17592186379506 1110 #uuid "6eada532-6efd-5f53-b0e2-e4af31829129" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379507 1034 "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{1}^{1-x/4-y}f(x,y,z)\\,dz]dA" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379507 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536016 true] [17592186379507 1111 #uuid "b8d91a9d-c94f-59b2-899c-43e061632bfb" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379508 1034 "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{1}^{1+4x}f(x,y,z)\\,dz]dA" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379508 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536016 true] [17592186379508 1111 #uuid "e82becf2-4775-5741-b54e-9423249b90f9" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379509 1034 "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{1+4x}f(x,y,z)\\,dz]dA" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379509 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536016 true] [17592186379509 1111 #uuid "ef125ac7-dff2-5108-8226-054cce41290a" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379510 1034 "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{1-x/4-y}f(x,y,z)\\,dz]dA" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379510 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536016 true] [17592186379510 1111 #uuid "8bec214f-809b-585a-8562-48908e2fde0e" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379511 1034 "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{1-y}f(x,y,z)\\,dz]dA" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379511 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536016 true] [17592186379511 1111 #uuid "7ce389b3-476f-5b37-902b-8ee51e85a52e" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379512 1017 #edb/ref 17592186379513 13194139536016 true] [17592186379512 1089 "q-162400" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379512 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536016 true] [17592186379512 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/bd69636822b7a3f68805273a4ffd72c7011a4f945a3ebbac8dcd99191e7ea730.png)\n\nChange the order of integration in the integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{(3-x)/3}{\\int }_{0}^{3-x-3y}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nso that it is integrated first with respect to $x,$ then over a region $D$ in the $yz$-plane. The integration region $R$ is shown above." 13194139536016 true] [17592186379512 1113 #uuid "6bfd9cc0-6de0-5fe8-89c3-170f6830fede" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379512 1145 "We wish to find the limits of integration ${u}_{1}(y,z)$ and ${u}_{2}(y,z)$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{(3-x)/3}{\\int }_{0}^{3-x-3y}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(y,z)}^{{u}_{2}(y,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the region of integration $R$ can be written as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{3-x}{3},\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 3-x-3y}\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$ to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}A,$ we write $R$ as a type II region. \n\nTo visualize this, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $x$-axis and in the direction of increasing $x,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/d26a7cc2c643b300226136a223be0fdebed4e17c99c2e562d1cc209caa023ea5.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the back boundary and leaves through the front boundary:\n\n- The front boundary of the region, written in the form $x=x(y,z),$ is\n\n$$\nz=3-x-3y\\,⟹\\,x=3-3y-z.\n$$\n\n- The back boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nx=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the type II representation of $R$ is \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le 3-3y-z},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/5641e6fc83054a355a2010084e1c8be2cd2d8144eae2aad0706704247f03b258.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration so that it is integrated first with respect to $x$ and then over $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{(3-x)/3}{\\int }_{0}^{3-x-3y}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{3-3y-z}f(x,y,z)\\,dx]dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379513 1000 #edb/ref 17592186379514 13194139536016 true] [17592186379513 1000 #edb/ref 17592186379515 13194139536016 true] [17592186379513 1000 #edb/ref 17592186379516 13194139536016 true] [17592186379513 1000 #edb/ref 17592186379517 13194139536016 true] [17592186379513 1000 #edb/ref 17592186379518 13194139536016 true] [17592186379513 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536016 true] [17592186379513 1041 "selection" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379513 1104 #edb/ref 17592186379517 13194139536016 true] [17592186379513 1110 #uuid "17307b0e-32f9-5c9e-a3cd-de96ba37d1e2" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379514 1034 "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{(y-z)/3}f(x,y,z)\\,dx]dA" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379514 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536016 true] [17592186379514 1111 #uuid "ad1db02d-bc60-5e15-9acf-f6469996ddb4" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379515 1034 "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{3-y+z}f(x,y,z)\\,dx]dA" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379515 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536016 true] [17592186379515 1111 #uuid "97b062bb-d0fc-5539-935f-15eb7f3aafbc" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379516 1034 "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{3}^{3-3y-z}f(x,y,z)\\,dx]dA" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379516 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536016 true] [17592186379516 1111 #uuid "947b1f2d-feae-539e-b8c0-e95131a24b0b" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379517 1034 "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{3-3y-z}f(x,y,z)\\,dx]dA" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379517 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536016 true] [17592186379517 1111 #uuid "75b9ba23-367a-50b6-81f9-04b4e7a27e1e" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379518 1034 "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{3}^{3-y+z}f(x,y,z)\\,dx]dA" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379518 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536016 true] [17592186379518 1111 #uuid "c94491e0-8ed6-5685-b08f-241c27ce49e5" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379519 1017 #edb/ref 17592186379520 13194139536016 true] [17592186379519 1089 "q-162481" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379519 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536016 true] [17592186379519 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/d76d928d43054c7d5c026cc25b95fb55855c7a8cbdb5fb35b5ae1f1d39a35c90.png)\n\nChange the order of integration in the integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{(y-1)^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nso that it is integrated first with respect to $y,$ then over a region $D$ in the $xz$-plane. The integration region $R$ is shown above." 13194139536016 true] [17592186379519 1113 #uuid "133c8185-f1c9-5cfa-88e2-7697817cdd72" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379519 1145 "We wish to find the limits of integration ${u}_{1}(x,z)$ and ${u}_{2}(x,z)$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{(y-1)^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,z)}^{{u}_{2}(x,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the region of integration $R$ can be written as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le (y-1)^{2}}\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$ to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}A,$ we write $R$ as a type III region. \n\nTo visualize this, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $y$-axis and in the direction of increasing $y,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/116ae6061410bca820b3c89869b23a36b5cef47e4fe483e390ee43d1e52ff726.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the back boundary and leaves through the front boundary:\n\n- The front boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x,z),$ is\n\n$$\nz=(y-1)^{2}\\,⟹\\,y=1-\\sqrt{z}.\n$$\n\n- The back boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the type III representation of $R$ is \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1-\\sqrt{z}},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/5613cb5b9718914e35f7259e3fe9dfd69ce128a861d19995e2c836c2b76d0f58.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration so that it is integrated first with respect to $y$ and then over $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{(y-1)^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{1-\\sqrt{z}}f(x,y,z)\\,dy]dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379520 1000 #edb/ref 17592186379521 13194139536016 true] [17592186379520 1000 #edb/ref 17592186379522 13194139536016 true] [17592186379520 1000 #edb/ref 17592186379523 13194139536016 true] [17592186379520 1000 #edb/ref 17592186379524 13194139536016 true] [17592186379520 1000 #edb/ref 17592186379525 13194139536016 true] [17592186379520 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536016 true] [17592186379520 1041 "selection" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379520 1104 #edb/ref 17592186379522 13194139536016 true] [17592186379520 1110 #uuid "a1e6139a-8c58-5abe-a0f2-789deafcee91" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379521 1034 "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{1}^{1-{z}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}A" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379521 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536016 true] [17592186379521 1111 #uuid "ab5cf20f-f199-5f62-ba4d-691ddaebbeb3" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379522 1034 "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{1-\\sqrt{z}}f(x,y,z)\\,dy]dA" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379522 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536016 true] [17592186379522 1111 #uuid "ffaf36a6-1c2c-5150-bf59-28b08fbf1555" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379523 1034 "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{{z}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}A" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379523 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536016 true] [17592186379523 1111 #uuid "9bbc553d-3c53-5d3d-bb2a-22ed0e2b451d" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379524 1034 "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{z}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}A" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379524 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536016 true] [17592186379524 1111 #uuid "37f165cf-6bac-54bc-ad3e-32cfab5ee1a4" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379525 1034 "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{\\sqrt{z}-1}^{1}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}A" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379525 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536016 true] [17592186379525 1111 #uuid "5ee5118e-4a44-5ea6-9a38-4c30c8c34c2e" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379526 1017 #edb/ref 17592186379527 13194139536016 true] [17592186379526 1089 "q-162482" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379526 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536016 true] [17592186379526 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/192cb96645cef0340e99769af3126b9d7e3775ed419e8414e3ad1ed683e919e3.png)\n\nChange the order of integration in the integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nso that it is integrated first with respect to $x,$ then $z,$ and then $y.$ The integration region $R$ is shown above." 13194139536016 true] [17592186379526 1113 #uuid "f03b73ae-4f63-5979-88b7-c08aa4b95288" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379526 1145 "We wish to find limits of integration ${u}_{1}(y,z),$ ${u}_{2}(y,z),$ ${v}_{1}(y),$ ${v}_{2}(y),$ $a,$ and $b$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(y,z)}^{{u}_{2}(y,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the region of integration $R$ can be written as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 2x,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1-x}\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$ to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y,$ we write $R$ as a type II region and its projection $D$ onto the $yz$-plane as a type I *plane* region.\n\nTo visualize this, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $x$-axis and in the direction of increasing $x,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/60f61df1520d366f6cbcac0b87e7e5eb453e8f011ff046b29a61cdae41624ddf.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the rear boundary and leaves through the front boundary:\n\n- The front boundary of the region, written in the form $x=x(y,z),$ is\n\n$$\nz=1-x\\,⟹\\,x=1-z.\n$$\n\n- The rear boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny=2x\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{y}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the type II representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,\\,\\frac{y}{2}\\le x\\le 1-z},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/02bbf0de5dad7ff72970ef5a939ac66fd05dfc06c31a23c37b6204042e1859b8.png)\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ as a type I plane region in the $yz$-plane with the top curve given by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=1-z \\\\ x=\\frac{y}{2}\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{y}{2}=1-z\\,⟹\\,z=1-\\frac{y}{2}.\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/2eea77ae3d1ae7a459dba98c98b20fabbec6b1d3a6cacb718b32688bd4ad11d7.png)\n\nHence, we have that\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1-\\frac{y}{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration so that it is integrated first with respect to $x,$ then $z,$ and then $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1-y/2}{\\int }_{y/2}^{1-z}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379527 1000 #edb/ref 17592186379528 13194139536016 true] [17592186379527 1000 #edb/ref 17592186379529 13194139536016 true] [17592186379527 1000 #edb/ref 17592186379530 13194139536016 true] [17592186379527 1000 #edb/ref 17592186379531 13194139536016 true] [17592186379527 1000 #edb/ref 17592186379532 13194139536016 true] [17592186379527 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536016 true] [17592186379527 1041 "selection" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379527 1104 #edb/ref 17592186379528 13194139536016 true] [17592186379527 1110 #uuid "4ac1447d-435b-56b8-8afe-3b5c7e418ca1" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379528 1034 "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1-y/2}{\\int }_{y/2}^{1-z}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379528 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536016 true] [17592186379528 1111 #uuid "8663d08e-227a-5b32-b410-ee4fc0676845" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379529 1034 "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1-2y}{\\int }_{2y}^{1-z/2}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379529 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536016 true] [17592186379529 1111 #uuid "cac70e86-1bdd-5b9f-bbc8-42ab51e6ffde" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379530 1034 "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1-y}{\\int }_{y/2}^{1-2z}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379530 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536016 true] [17592186379530 1111 #uuid "7d2c3b89-84a2-5c30-94d6-23d2ea7390e0" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379531 1034 "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1-y/2}{\\int }_{1}^{1+z}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379531 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536016 true] [17592186379531 1111 #uuid "cfcab143-c79a-53e6-9d77-b107b1359e76" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379532 1034 "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{y}{\\int }_{1}^{1-z}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379532 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536016 true] [17592186379532 1111 #uuid "fc4a148d-b73c-5bab-a07c-f708560b88f3" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379533 1017 #edb/ref 17592186379534 13194139536016 true] [17592186379533 1089 "q-162488" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379533 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536016 true] [17592186379533 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/380b44a8baf8ed15eb3eb4e66e77b820f5bc51a73be840526db67d96f6d763ed.png)\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{y/2}\\frac{1}{(1+{x}^{2})(1-x)}\\,dx\\,dy\\,dz.$ The region of integration is shown above.\n\n*Hint: Change the order of integration to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z.$*" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379533 1113 #uuid "df891a57-61b8-59e7-aa5b-534b4f8e90c5" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379533 1145 "We cannot integrate the given integral because we cannot find a simple antiderivative of the integrand. So, we change the order of integration.\n\nWe wish to find the limits of integration ${u}_{1}(x,z),$ ${u}_{2}(x,z),$ ${v}_{1}(z),$ ${v}_{2}(z),$ $a,$ and $b$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{y/2}\\frac{1}{(1+{x}^{2})(1-x)}\\,dx\\,dy\\,dz={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(z)}^{{v}_{2}(z)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,z)}^{{u}_{2}(x,z)}\\frac{1}{(1+{x}^{2})(1-x)}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the region of integration $R$ can be written as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2}y}\n$$\n\nThe region $R$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/185f85445f95382afdd9516351f0cce3c4d2b11d99f65da2d5a36db0fc853215.png)\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z$ to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z,$ we write $R$ as a type III region and its projection $D$ onto the $xz$-plane as a type II *plane* region.\n\nTo visualize this, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $y$-axis and in the direction of increasing $y,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/6aeba17b071bd275be7147ffb925b488881a2fb8de5f544dbffe8caab5c036e4.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the left boundary and leaves through the right boundary:\n\n- The left boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x,z),$ is\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}y\\,⟹\\,y=2x.\n$$\n\n- The right boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the type III representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,\\,2x\\le y\\le 2},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/be089ef60080ad9181db8a5dacccf6abe382bef492369342fa15761134b507d2.png)\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ as a type II plane region in the $xz$-plane with the right curve given by $x=\\frac{1}{2}(2)=1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/885b884fee574cae3c2f62ac0092e64b9be1731ae8510f6e910d0a101ffa3f20.png)\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{y/2}\\frac{1}{(1+{x}^{2})(1-x)}\\,dx\\,dy\\,dz & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2x}^{2}\\frac{1}{(1+{x}^{2})(1-x)}\\,dy\\,dx\\,dz \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{(1+{x}^{2})(1-x)}[{\\int }_{2x}^{2}\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{(1+{x}^{2})(1-x)}[y]_{2x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2-2x}{(1+{x}^{2})(1-x)}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2(1-x)}{(1+{x}^{2})(1-x)}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}[z]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379534 1000 #edb/ref 17592186379535 13194139536016 true] [17592186379534 1000 #edb/ref 17592186379536 13194139536016 true] [17592186379534 1000 #edb/ref 17592186379537 13194139536016 true] [17592186379534 1000 #edb/ref 17592186379538 13194139536016 true] [17592186379534 1000 #edb/ref 17592186379539 13194139536016 true] [17592186379534 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536016 true] [17592186379534 1041 "selection" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379534 1104 #edb/ref 17592186379537 13194139536016 true] [17592186379534 1110 #uuid "14340785-a496-5f6c-bc94-7885ea7befbc" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379535 1034 "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379535 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536016 true] [17592186379535 1111 #uuid "13cef9f3-2cbe-55cb-8482-9d01db8496ad" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379536 1034 "\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379536 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536016 true] [17592186379536 1111 #uuid "26db765d-9df2-5ab9-aabc-e7da9a149933" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379537 1034 "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379537 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536016 true] [17592186379537 1111 #uuid "be10a8bc-8a53-50d2-ad26-415d18f394d3" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379538 1034 "2\\pi " 13194139536016 true] [17592186379538 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536016 true] [17592186379538 1111 #uuid "1b2de698-e1ce-5a5d-be85-d210be2ec97a" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379539 1034 "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379539 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536016 true] [17592186379539 1111 #uuid "fc1362e0-244a-598a-a3a7-d59b8cee24b1" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379540 1017 #edb/ref 17592186379541 13194139536016 true] [17592186379540 1089 "q-162489" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379540 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536016 true] [17592186379540 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/7c959b9c517b17d9427de450def510b9673d91fd951e28696d4d56d8a768b74d.png)\n\nChange the order of integration in the integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\nso that it is integrated first with respect to $y,$ then $x,$ and then $z.$ The integration region $R$ is shown above." 13194139536016 true] [17592186379540 1113 #uuid "51e3e7e7-44a6-54ed-a3c2-de6f58c215e2" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379540 1145 "We wish to find limits of integration ${u}_{1}(x,z),$ ${u}_{2}(x,z),$ ${v}_{1}(z),$ ${v}_{2}(z),$ $a,$ and $b$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(z)}^{{v}_{2}(z)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,z)}^{{u}_{2}(x,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the region of integration $R$ can be written as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{2},\\,\\,0\\le x\\le {y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z$ to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z,$ we write $R$ as a type III region and its projection $D$ onto the $xz$-plane as a type II *plane* region.\n\nTo visualize this, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $y$-axis and in the direction of increasing $y,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/c3837a3551b35a5146fd2c8e2441e4a9d6d947e8c52e44254d48eb854e54e605.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the left boundary and leaves through the right boundary:\n\n- The left boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x,z),$ is\n\n$$\nx={y}^{2}\\,⟹\\,y=\\sqrt{x}.\n$$\n\n- The right boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the type III representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,\\,\\sqrt{x}\\le y\\le \\sqrt{2}},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/22581a56f4d9cb611bd9a58b06b1036cd47b500217570ae6890d0f38dba6742c.png)\n\nWe now consider the projection $D$ as a type II plane region in the $xz$-plane with the right curve given by \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=\\sqrt{x} \\\\ y=\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\sqrt{x}=\\sqrt{2}\\,⟹\\,x=2.\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/e2305d3da63886703666fd58a2adfe8f15f2eceb7261922f46ba4949b0fc0cde.png)\n\nHence, we have that\n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration so that it is integrated first with respect to $y,$ then $x,$ then $z,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{x}}^{\\sqrt{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379541 1000 #edb/ref 17592186379542 13194139536016 true] [17592186379541 1000 #edb/ref 17592186379543 13194139536016 true] [17592186379541 1000 #edb/ref 17592186379544 13194139536016 true] [17592186379541 1000 #edb/ref 17592186379545 13194139536016 true] [17592186379541 1000 #edb/ref 17592186379546 13194139536016 true] [17592186379541 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536016 true] [17592186379541 1041 "selection" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379541 1104 #edb/ref 17592186379543 13194139536016 true] [17592186379541 1110 #uuid "a1309f76-71f8-526e-8be3-9795b9754fcd" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379542 1034 "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}{\\int }_{x}^{\\sqrt{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379542 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536016 true] [17592186379542 1111 #uuid "54da0d3a-37ff-5da3-a7ab-ff4a86fa3803" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379543 1034 "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{x}}^{\\sqrt{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379543 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536016 true] [17592186379543 1111 #uuid "67af4759-3e72-5401-95f4-301081967371" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379544 1034 "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{x}^{2x}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379544 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536016 true] [17592186379544 1111 #uuid "f4207370-0893-51ad-91d0-e2362b7e8b4f" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379545 1034 "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{x}}^{2}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379545 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536016 true] [17592186379545 1111 #uuid "c04e6654-b0ab-5ffa-aebd-2c79ed568e94" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379546 1034 "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{x}}^{2}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\t