3c23c2af628f18dfaf16695f9ec5beac68b0d16ce93d6a993d6fb8c.png)\n\nThen, the region $R$ is shown in the diagram below, where $\\sqrt{x}\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/cdc8fb2c7f6ef9a28b01094ef3bf18089297a6ef9a431c5152311e5e92cd2557.png)\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z$ to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z,$ we write $R$ as a type II region and its projection $D$ onto the $yz$-plane as a type II *plane* region.\n\nTo visualize this, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $x$-axis and in the direction of increasing $x,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/d805df26c5a471323d855194b41e052142e711e7f7f9ddf910d792ae74cbc2c6.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the back boundary and leaves through the front boundary:\n\n- The back boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nx=0.\n$$\n\n- The front boundary of the region, written in the form $x=x(y,z),$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{x}\\,⟹\\,x={y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the type II representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le {y}^{2}},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/4d40e420abeec633cd145dca33071d6459e4b4009e4c6cfc871d6c6150be51e4.png)\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ as a type II plane region in the $yz$-plane with the left curve given by $y=\\sqrt{0}=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/81782a6c847e5369dfed7524619de354ff7676a856685fb4340d09cf61dca339.png)\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\,0\\le z\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{{\\pi }^{2}/4}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{x}}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{z}{y}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{y})\\,dy\\,dx\\,dz & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\frac{z}{y}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{y})\\,dx\\,dy\\,dz \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{z}{y}[{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{y})\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{z}{y}{[y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{y})]}_{x=0}^{x={y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{z}{y}(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-0)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}z\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}z\\,\\text{d}z]\\cdot [{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y] \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}{z}^{2}]}_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\cdot [-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}-0)\\cdot (-0+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379548 1000 #edb/ref 17592186379549 13194139536016 true] [17592186379548 1000 #edb/ref 17592186379550 13194139536016 true] [17592186379548 1000 #edb/ref 17592186379551 13194139536016 true] [17592186379548 1000 #edb/ref 17592186379552 13194139536016 true] [17592186379548 1000 #edb/ref 17592186379553 13194139536016 true] [17592186379548 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536016 true] [17592186379548 1041 "selection" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379548 1104 #edb/ref 17592186379550 13194139536016 true] [17592186379548 1110 #uuid "8373aa6b-ddd2-54c8-870a-18ed8ade520d" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379549 1034 "{\\pi }^{2}-1" 13194139536016 true] [17592186379549 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536016 true] [17592186379549 1111 #uuid "5485e981-ff4f-5aa9-8652-76f0566871b8" 13194139536016 true]